detail.abstract:
In 1920, Ludwig von Mises demonstrated that socialist planning cannot work because without market prices, rational economic calculation is impossible.&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper argues that legal systems face an analogous problem. Legal actors must make decisions based on information dispersed across millions of individuals: How will courts interpret "reasonable time"? What is the actual compliance rate for a given regulation? Legal systems generate this information through caseby-case application, each producing a "legal price," but lack mechanisms to aggregate and transmit it. The result is massive uncertainty.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This framework illuminates several pathologies. Normative inflation occurs when rules proliferate beyond enforcement capacity, diluting expected compliance. Normative deflation occurs when constitutional rigidity prevents adaptation. Some systems exhibit normative stagflation: simultaneous inflation of unenforced rules and deflation of constitutional adaptability. Argentina exemplifies this condition.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The analysis suggests legal systems need mechanisms performing for normative interpretation what prices perform for economic coordination. I examine possible implementations and their tradeoffs.
</div>
